1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,320
You have rifles, cannon and rockets.

2
00:00:02,870 --> 00:00:04,580
They have mostly spears and shields.

3
00:00:05,450 --> 00:00:07,120
By 3:30 in the afternoon,

4
00:00:07,310 --> 00:00:08,280
your camp is gone.

5
00:00:08,790 --> 00:00:09,460
What did you miss?

6
00:00:10,363 --> 00:00:14,663
January 22, 1879. Isandlwana,

7
00:00:14,663 --> 00:00:16,013
in the Zulu Kingdom,

8
00:00:16,263 --> 00:00:17,133
southern Africa.

9
00:00:17,672 --> 00:00:19,872
This is the story of the battle that shocked

10
00:00:19,872 --> 00:00:22,692
the British Empire, told from the side of

11
00:00:22,692 --> 00:00:24,082
the man who planned it.

12
00:00:24,837 --> 00:00:26,087
It starts under a tree.

13
00:00:26,977 --> 00:00:29,467
On December 11, 1878,

14
00:00:29,817 --> 00:00:31,307
beside the Thukela River,

15
00:00:31,677 --> 00:00:34,077
British officials read an ultimatum to the

16
00:00:34,077 --> 00:00:35,567
Zulu king's envoys.

17
00:00:36,192 --> 00:00:38,592
It came from Sir Bartle Frere,

18
00:00:38,592 --> 00:00:40,332
Britain's High Commissioner in southern

19
00:00:40,332 --> 00:00:43,232
Africa, and he sent it without London's

20
00:00:43,232 --> 00:00:43,662
approval.

21
00:00:44,296 --> 00:00:47,676
13 demands. Hand over men accused in border

22
00:00:47,676 --> 00:00:50,386
incidents. Pay a fine in cattle.

23
00:00:50,756 --> 00:00:51,986
Accept a British agent.

24
00:00:52,596 --> 00:00:54,956
And the one no king could accept: disband

25
00:00:54,956 --> 00:00:56,366
the Zulu army.

26
00:00:57,026 --> 00:00:58,196
Even at the time,

27
00:00:58,206 --> 00:00:59,476
the excuse looked thin.

28
00:01:00,166 --> 00:01:02,646
The ultimatum rested on three border incidents

29
00:01:02,646 --> 00:01:06,696
in 1878. Flimsy grounds for an invasion.

30
00:01:07,589 --> 00:01:10,389
The deadline ran out on January 10,

31
00:01:10,389 --> 00:01:13,019
1879. The next day,

32
00:01:13,129 --> 00:01:15,578
British columns crossed into Zululand.

33
00:01:15,848 --> 00:01:18,868
Three columns invaded: one along the coast,

34
00:01:18,868 --> 00:01:20,078
one in the north,

35
00:01:20,308 --> 00:01:21,808
and one through the centre,

36
00:01:21,808 --> 00:01:23,638
across the river at Rorke's Drift.

37
00:01:24,329 --> 00:01:25,879
The main one, the Centre Column,

38
00:01:26,169 --> 00:01:28,619
was led by the British commander himself,

39
00:01:28,709 --> 00:01:32,369
Lord Chelmsford. Around 4,700 men,

40
00:01:32,369 --> 00:01:34,559
with ox wagons, rifles,

41
00:01:35,249 --> 00:01:36,359
field guns and rockets.

42
00:01:37,289 --> 00:01:39,219
Across the border, at his capital,

43
00:01:39,389 --> 00:01:42,269
oNdini, sat King Cetshwayo.

44
00:01:42,269 --> 00:01:43,899
Even after the invasion,

45
00:01:44,129 --> 00:01:46,189
he would send messengers asking for peace

46
00:01:46,189 --> 00:01:49,279
talks. But first, he called up his army.

47
00:01:49,948 --> 00:01:52,368
To lead it, he chose a man most people have

48
00:01:52,368 --> 00:01:56,296
never heard of. Ntshingwayo kaMahole Khoza.

49
00:01:56,696 --> 00:01:58,486
He was about 70 years old,

50
00:01:58,656 --> 00:02:00,836
a senior commander and councillor of the

51
00:02:00,836 --> 00:02:03,796
king. Only two photographs of him are known

52
00:02:03,796 --> 00:02:06,146
to exist. This is one of them.

53
00:02:06,887 --> 00:02:09,327
His army: around 20,000 warriors,

54
00:02:09,327 --> 00:02:11,417
in regiments grouped by age,

55
00:02:11,847 --> 00:02:14,327
each with its own shield colours.

56
00:02:14,327 --> 00:02:16,607
Most carried a short stabbing spear and

57
00:02:16,607 --> 00:02:19,917
a cowhide shield. Some carried old firearms.

58
00:02:20,943 --> 00:02:23,783
The king's order, as it was recorded: "March

59
00:02:23,783 --> 00:02:25,523
slowly, attack at dawn,

60
00:02:25,523 --> 00:02:27,193
and eat up the red soldiers."

61
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:31,130
Ntshingwayo's army marched in two columns,

62
00:02:31,720 --> 00:02:33,110
within sight of each other,

63
00:02:33,380 --> 00:02:35,660
with scouts out front and screening parties

64
00:02:35,660 --> 00:02:39,250
of 200 to 400 men hiding the main body.

65
00:02:39,786 --> 00:02:42,316
In five days, it covered more than 50 miles.

66
00:02:43,146 --> 00:02:45,726
In ten days, Chelmsford's column had moved

67
00:02:45,726 --> 00:02:47,146
just over ten.

68
00:02:47,546 --> 00:02:49,956
And the British had no idea where it was.

69
00:02:50,825 --> 00:02:53,865
On January 20, Chelmsford made camp beneath

70
00:02:53,865 --> 00:02:55,885
a strange, sphinx-shaped mountain.

71
00:02:55,885 --> 00:02:57,680
Isandlwana.

72
00:02:58,080 --> 00:03:00,220
His own standing orders said to entrench

73
00:03:00,220 --> 00:03:03,260
every camp, or circle the wagons into a

74
00:03:03,260 --> 00:03:06,250
laager. At Isandlwana, he did neither.

75
00:03:06,837 --> 00:03:09,697
His reason? "It would take a week." The

76
00:03:09,697 --> 00:03:12,217
camp was temporary, the wagons were needed

77
00:03:12,217 --> 00:03:14,207
for supplies, and above all,

78
00:03:14,437 --> 00:03:16,417
he did not believe the Zulus would dare

79
00:03:16,417 --> 00:03:17,267
attack it.

80
00:03:17,754 --> 00:03:21,214
On January 21, the Zulu army slipped into

81
00:03:21,214 --> 00:03:24,394
the Ngwebeni Valley, about seven miles from

82
00:03:24,394 --> 00:03:24,944
the camp.

83
00:03:25,604 --> 00:03:28,084
20,000 men, sitting in total quiet,

84
00:03:28,084 --> 00:03:30,074
waiting for their day to attack.

85
00:03:30,704 --> 00:03:32,220
January 23.

86
00:03:32,750 --> 00:03:34,740
Before dawn on the 22nd,

87
00:03:35,050 --> 00:03:37,170
Chelmsford marched out toward the Mangeni

88
00:03:37,170 --> 00:03:38,440
hills, to the south-east,

89
00:03:39,250 --> 00:03:40,940
where Zulu groups had been seen.

90
00:03:41,790 --> 00:03:44,100
He took about 2,800 men,

91
00:03:44,430 --> 00:03:46,140
including half his British infantry.

92
00:03:46,873 --> 00:03:49,313
Some historians argue those Zulu groups

93
00:03:49,313 --> 00:03:50,353
were there on purpose,

94
00:03:50,353 --> 00:03:51,943
to pull the British away.

95
00:03:52,673 --> 00:03:53,903
If so, it worked.

96
00:03:54,773 --> 00:03:56,633
More than half of Chelmsford's force was

97
00:03:56,633 --> 00:03:58,203
now miles from the camp.

98
00:03:58,952 --> 00:04:02,552
Left behind at Isandlwana: just over 1,300

99
00:04:02,552 --> 00:04:04,492
men and two field guns,

100
00:04:04,492 --> 00:04:07,712
under Lieutenant Colonel Henry Pulleine,

101
00:04:07,712 --> 00:04:09,992
an administrator with no front-line command

102
00:04:09,992 --> 00:04:10,982
experience.

103
00:04:11,733 --> 00:04:13,413
His orders: defend the camp,

104
00:04:13,413 --> 00:04:14,043
and wait.

105
00:04:14,769 --> 00:04:16,139
From early that morning,

106
00:04:16,269 --> 00:04:18,689
lookouts reported Zulus on the hills to

107
00:04:18,689 --> 00:04:22,139
the north-east. Pulleine sent word to Chelmsford.

108
00:04:22,569 --> 00:04:24,439
Chelmsford did not turn back.

109
00:04:25,115 --> 00:04:28,235
At about 10:30, Colonel Anthony Durnford

110
00:04:28,235 --> 00:04:31,395
arrived with about 500 African troops and

111
00:04:31,395 --> 00:04:33,805
a rocket battery. He was senior,

112
00:04:34,175 --> 00:04:36,005
but he did not take over the camp.

113
00:04:36,552 --> 00:04:38,722
Instead, he decided to ride out,

114
00:04:39,052 --> 00:04:41,712
after a Zulu force he believed was threatening

115
00:04:41,712 --> 00:04:42,962
Chelmsford's rear.

116
00:04:43,643 --> 00:04:46,183
Around 11, a troop of his mounted scouts

117
00:04:46,183 --> 00:04:48,663
under Lieutenant Charles Raw chased a few

118
00:04:48,663 --> 00:04:50,673
Zulus across the high ground.

119
00:04:51,203 --> 00:04:52,663
They reached the edge of a valley,

120
00:04:52,663 --> 00:04:53,693
and looked down.

121
00:04:54,327 --> 00:04:56,637
Below them sat the main Zulu army.

122
00:04:57,715 --> 00:04:58,795
The secret was out,

123
00:04:58,795 --> 00:05:01,855
a day early. The regiments rose and poured

124
00:05:01,855 --> 00:05:02,845
out of the valley.

125
00:05:03,295 --> 00:05:05,325
There was no time to form up as planned.

126
00:05:05,935 --> 00:05:07,255
They deployed on the run,

127
00:05:07,255 --> 00:05:09,685
in the reverse of their intended order.

128
00:05:10,381 --> 00:05:12,901
Even so, they fell into the classic Zulu

129
00:05:12,901 --> 00:05:16,261
battle formation: the horns and chest of

130
00:05:16,261 --> 00:05:16,731
the buffalo.

131
00:05:17,671 --> 00:05:20,121
The chest, 7,000 to 9,000 men,

132
00:05:20,411 --> 00:05:22,631
drives straight at the enemy and pins him

133
00:05:22,631 --> 00:05:23,221
in place.

134
00:05:23,857 --> 00:05:26,907
The two horns, thousands of fast young warriors,

135
00:05:27,397 --> 00:05:30,227
run wide around both sides to surround him.

136
00:05:30,646 --> 00:05:33,486
And behind them, the loins: a reserve of

137
00:05:33,486 --> 00:05:36,596
4,000 to 5,000, waiting to finish the job.

138
00:05:37,326 --> 00:05:39,756
The right horn swung wide to the north.

139
00:05:40,346 --> 00:05:42,526
The left horn raced to wrap around the British

140
00:05:42,526 --> 00:05:43,016
right.

141
00:05:43,796 --> 00:05:46,376
Pulleine sent six companies of the 24th

142
00:05:46,376 --> 00:05:48,966
Regiment out into a long firing line,

143
00:05:49,296 --> 00:05:50,426
far from the tents.

144
00:05:51,088 --> 00:05:53,198
The Martini-Henry rifle was deadly.

145
00:05:54,188 --> 00:05:56,228
Volley after volley pinned the Zulu chest

146
00:05:56,228 --> 00:05:58,478
in the grass for an hour or more.

147
00:05:59,068 --> 00:06:00,148
The warriors lay flat,

148
00:06:00,148 --> 00:06:01,318
and crept forward.

149
00:06:01,852 --> 00:06:03,802
The guns fired over their heads.

150
00:06:04,512 --> 00:06:06,772
The rocket battery got off three rockets

151
00:06:06,772 --> 00:06:08,082
before it was overrun.

152
00:06:09,032 --> 00:06:11,212
Only one in ten of the African auxiliaries

153
00:06:11,212 --> 00:06:12,542
even had a firearm.

154
00:06:13,338 --> 00:06:14,988
And the line was too long,

155
00:06:15,338 --> 00:06:17,608
too thin, and too far from the camp.

156
00:06:18,458 --> 00:06:20,148
As one historian puts it,

157
00:06:20,378 --> 00:06:22,458
the British tried to defend too large a

158
00:06:22,458 --> 00:06:22,868
perimeter.

159
00:06:23,671 --> 00:06:25,851
On the right, Durnford's men fought from

160
00:06:25,851 --> 00:06:28,631
a dry riverbed, a donga.

161
00:06:28,631 --> 00:06:29,841
Far from the wagons,

162
00:06:30,111 --> 00:06:31,701
they began to run out of ammunition.

163
00:06:32,631 --> 00:06:35,151
For a century, one story explained the whole

164
00:06:35,151 --> 00:06:38,551
defeat: ammunition boxes screwed shut with

165
00:06:38,551 --> 00:06:41,531
rusty screws, and too few screwdrivers to

166
00:06:41,531 --> 00:06:42,241
open them.

167
00:06:42,705 --> 00:06:45,055
Modern historians are not so sure.

168
00:06:45,805 --> 00:06:48,305
Some argue the main firing line never ran

169
00:06:48,305 --> 00:06:50,715
out. The men far from the camp,

170
00:06:50,845 --> 00:06:52,695
like Durnford's, probably did.

171
00:06:53,609 --> 00:06:56,049
Durnford pulled back. That left the flank

172
00:06:56,049 --> 00:06:57,609
of the regulars wide open.

173
00:06:57,609 --> 00:07:00,269
Pulleine ordered the line back toward the

174
00:07:00,269 --> 00:07:00,679
camp.

175
00:07:01,247 --> 00:07:03,387
That was the moment the chest had been waiting

176
00:07:03,387 --> 00:07:06,317
for. It rose out of the grass and charged.

177
00:07:07,251 --> 00:07:09,591
One older warrior stood up in the open and

178
00:07:09,591 --> 00:07:11,891
shouted to the young men: "Don't you dare

179
00:07:11,891 --> 00:07:13,771
run away. Your leader,

180
00:07:13,771 --> 00:07:16,621
Cetshwayo, never told you to run away."

181
00:07:17,477 --> 00:07:19,037
And then, at 2:30,

182
00:07:19,037 --> 00:07:20,147
the sky went wrong.

183
00:07:20,970 --> 00:07:24,510
A solar eclipse. As one Zulu warrior remembered

184
00:07:24,510 --> 00:07:26,870
it: "The sun turned black in the middle

185
00:07:26,870 --> 00:07:27,580
of the battle."

186
00:07:28,312 --> 00:07:29,592
In the strange half-light,

187
00:07:29,592 --> 00:07:32,382
the horns closed. The camp was surrounded.

188
00:07:33,156 --> 00:07:36,116
Miles away, a colonial officer saw the camp

189
00:07:36,116 --> 00:07:38,426
from a distance and sent a desperate message:

190
00:07:39,216 --> 00:07:40,706
"For God's sake come back.

191
00:07:41,296 --> 00:07:42,386
The camp is surrounded,

192
00:07:42,736 --> 00:07:44,626
and things I fear are going badly."

193
00:07:45,465 --> 00:07:47,525
The fighting swept through the tents and

194
00:07:47,525 --> 00:07:49,595
the wagons, hand to hand.

195
00:07:50,145 --> 00:07:52,665
Small groups of soldiers stood back to back,

196
00:07:52,665 --> 00:07:54,895
until there was nothing left to fire.

197
00:07:55,565 --> 00:07:57,735
By 3:30, the camp had fallen.

198
00:07:58,876 --> 00:08:01,166
Survivors ran for the Buffalo River,

199
00:08:01,556 --> 00:08:04,136
down a rocky path later called Fugitives'

200
00:08:04,136 --> 00:08:04,486
Drift.

201
00:08:05,253 --> 00:08:07,173
Lieutenants Melvill and Coghill tried to

202
00:08:07,173 --> 00:08:09,983
save the Queen's Colour of the 24th.

203
00:08:10,453 --> 00:08:11,553
They lost it in the river,

204
00:08:11,553 --> 00:08:13,603
and were killed on the far bank.

205
00:08:14,333 --> 00:08:16,853
In 1907, they were awarded the Victoria

206
00:08:16,853 --> 00:08:17,603
Cross.

207
00:08:18,546 --> 00:08:20,176
Not everyone saw heroes.

208
00:08:21,026 --> 00:08:23,466
Sir Garnet Wolseley wrote: "I don't like

209
00:08:23,466 --> 00:08:26,326
the idea of officers escaping on horseback

210
00:08:26,326 --> 00:08:28,476
when their men on foot are being killed."

211
00:08:29,342 --> 00:08:31,842
More than 1,300 men of the British force

212
00:08:31,842 --> 00:08:35,592
died. Only five Imperial officers survived.

213
00:08:36,514 --> 00:08:39,154
The Zulu army paid too.

214
00:08:39,154 --> 00:08:41,294
Somewhere between 1,000 and 3,000 warriors

215
00:08:41,294 --> 00:08:46,396
were killed. Two of Ntshingwayo's own sons

216
00:08:46,396 --> 00:08:46,626
were badly wounded.

217
00:08:46,887 --> 00:08:49,427
At sundown, Chelmsford marched back into

218
00:08:49,427 --> 00:08:52,317
the silent camp. His words were recorded:

219
00:08:52,887 --> 00:08:55,327
"But I left over 1,000 men to guard the

220
00:08:55,327 --> 00:08:55,757
camp."

221
00:08:56,563 --> 00:08:58,853
His column spent the night on the battlefield,

222
00:08:59,323 --> 00:09:01,723
among the dead, and marched away before

223
00:09:01,723 --> 00:09:02,153
sunrise.

224
00:09:03,220 --> 00:09:05,700
That same night, Zulu reserves crossed the

225
00:09:05,700 --> 00:09:07,600
river and attacked the little mission station

226
00:09:07,600 --> 00:09:11,480
at Rorke's Drift. About 150 men held it

227
00:09:11,480 --> 00:09:14,280
until morning. Britain needed that story,

228
00:09:14,280 --> 00:09:15,670
and it told it loudly.

229
00:09:16,595 --> 00:09:19,075
Because Isandlwana was the British Army's

230
00:09:19,075 --> 00:09:22,335
worst defeat against an enemy with far simpler

231
00:09:22,335 --> 00:09:22,945
weapons.

232
00:09:23,740 --> 00:09:25,050
For the Zulu Kingdom,

233
00:09:25,360 --> 00:09:27,280
it would be the greatest victory of the

234
00:09:27,280 --> 00:09:28,050
whole war.

235
00:09:28,706 --> 00:09:32,236
Britain sent reinforcements. On July 4,

236
00:09:32,346 --> 00:09:36,506
1879, at Ulundi, a British square with Gatling

237
00:09:36,506 --> 00:09:40,236
guns broke the Zulu army in about 45 minutes.

238
00:09:40,826 --> 00:09:42,236
The royal capital burned.

239
00:09:43,019 --> 00:09:45,379
Cetshwayo was captured in August and sent

240
00:09:45,379 --> 00:09:48,239
to Cape Town. The Zulu Kingdom was broken

241
00:09:48,239 --> 00:09:48,909
apart.

242
00:09:49,611 --> 00:09:51,671
He even travelled to London to plead his

243
00:09:51,671 --> 00:09:53,821
case, and was briefly restored.

244
00:09:54,591 --> 00:09:56,321
He died in 1884.

245
00:09:57,215 --> 00:09:59,285
Ntshingwayo survived the war.

246
00:09:59,955 --> 00:10:03,425
In July 1883, in the civil war that followed,

247
00:10:03,755 --> 00:10:05,355
he was killed at oNdini,

248
00:10:05,355 --> 00:10:06,891
in his seventies.

249
00:10:07,421 --> 00:10:09,791
Not even his own side agreed on him.

250
00:10:10,261 --> 00:10:12,341
Some Zulus blamed him for how many young

251
00:10:12,341 --> 00:10:14,513
men died at Isandlwana.

252
00:10:15,043 --> 00:10:17,153
So, did he out-plan the British Empire?

253
00:10:18,063 --> 00:10:20,663
He hid 20,000 men seven miles from their

254
00:10:20,663 --> 00:10:22,943
camp, while the British split their own

255
00:10:22,943 --> 00:10:23,973
army in two.

256
00:10:24,588 --> 00:10:26,998
And when his plan was discovered a day early,

257
00:10:27,408 --> 00:10:29,268
his regiments adapted on the run,

258
00:10:29,268 --> 00:10:30,998
and still closed the horns.

259
00:10:31,821 --> 00:10:34,101
Chelmsford had rifles, cannon and rockets.

260
00:10:34,101 --> 00:10:37,811
Ntshingwayo had scouts, speed and surprise.

261
00:10:38,885 --> 00:10:41,495
Was Isandlwana won by Zulu planning,

262
00:10:41,965 --> 00:10:44,035
or lost by British overconfidence?

263
00:10:44,965 --> 00:10:45,725
Tell me in the comments,

264
00:10:45,725 --> 00:10:47,935
and subscribe to Buried Chronicles.

